partner register and interface results in recognition and long-term resilience agendas.                            |\n\n## 20. Concluding Notes\n\nOrmoc already has more partners than many cities: national agencies, utilities, a geothermal operator and five CSOs on its Council; a resilience partnership with the NRC and EDC; volunteers under agreement; tertiary institutions in a coordination group; and a record of helping its neighbours. What is missing is the standing arrangement that makes each relationship dependable on the worst night: a signed mutual aid agreement, a restoration-priority list, an interface agreement with each City office that owns part of the risk, and a register that shows which commitments are real.\n\nWriting these procedures does not create the partnerships. Until the partner register holds signed agreements, at least one mutual aid agreement is in force and has been exercised, the Part C lead offices have signed interface agreements, and the first partner forum has been held, this chapter remains a controlled working draft.\n\n## References\n\nThe official text governs.\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2023). *Ormoc City disaster risk reduction and management ten-year plan (2023–2032)*.\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2026a). *Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC institutional assessment tool* (Version 1.7) \\[Unpublished assessment workbook\\].\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2026b). *Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC master requirements traceability and 140-indicator data dictionary* (Version 1.2) \\[Unpublished project reference workbook\\].\n\nCity Government of Ormoc, City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2019–2025). *Resolutions on inter-LGU assistance, volunteers and partnerships* (Res. No. 05, s. 2019; No. 01, s. 2020; Nos. 10, 12–15, s. 2021; Nos. 03–04, s. 2022; Nos. 07 and 10, s. 2024; No. 04, s. 2025).\n\nCity Government of Ormoc, Office of the City Mayor. (2022–2024). *Executive Order on the City CCCM Team (2022); Executive Order Nos. 2023-0025, 2023-0026